# Your Python harness agent

This starter was bundled with temporal-agent-harness __HARNESS_VERSION__. It needs Python 3.11+,
uv, the Temporal CLI, and an OpenAI API key. Its weather tool returns sample data.

```bash
uv sync
cp env.example .env.local
# Set OPENAI_API_KEY in .env.local; never commit the key.
uv run temporal-agent-harness schema my_agent.workflow:OpenAIHelloAgentWorkflow -o agent.schema.json
uv run temporal-agent-harness skills install
```

Run these from this directory in separate terminals:

```bash
temporal server start-dev
uv run temporal-agent-harness session-manager
uv run temporal-agent-harness serve agents.toml
uv run --env-file .env.local python -m my_agent.worker
```

Skip the first command when your configured Temporal service already exists. The default is
localhost:7233, namespace default. Export the same connection settings in every terminal;
`.env.local` above loads only into the agent worker. Keep TASK_QUEUE_PREFIX consistent.

Open http://localhost:8000, start OpenAI Hello (Python), and send `ask` a weather question.
Expand the tool trace to see get_weather and its sample result. `ping` echoes a typed text
message without calling a model, useful for checking the worker connection. Reload the page
to reconnect. Temporal's own workflow UI is at http://localhost:8233.

Run `uv run temporal-agent-harness doctor --registry agents.toml` for read-only diagnostics.
After changing the agent, regenerate the schema and restart the worker; use a new session for
changed workflow behavior. Commit your schema and uv.lock. For application clients, consult
`.agents/skills/temporal-harness-python/references/api.md`.
